//! ui/theme — what a package's design tokens mean, decided by the app.
//!
//! A library that uses tokens declares its own closed vocabulary as an
//! ordinary enum and styles itself against that; the app that uses the library
//! closes the loop at mount with one theme per library, mapping that library's
//! tokens to its own or to raw values. Exhaustiveness is the whole contract:
//! when a library adds a token, every consumer fails to compile until its
//! theme maps it (`design/ui-reactivity.md` §Design tokens).
//!
//! **A `Theme` cannot be built yet, and that is deliberate rather than
//! unfinished.** The type is here because `mount` takes a list of them, and a
//! signature that gains a parameter later is a breaking change while a type
//! that gains a constructor later is not. What is missing is the other half of
//! tokens — `Color::Token(TokenReference)` in `ui/style`, and the resolution
//! step that turns a chain of them into a value at mount — so the only list a
//! program can pass today is the empty one, which is exactly what an app with
//! no tokens wants to pass. Until then, a colour is written out.

/// One package's tokens, resolved to values.
///
/// Opaque, and with no constructor: see the module note above.
export struct Theme(Int);
